  "profile": {
    "video_id": "iron-snail:yw_dT1WgQQg",
    "purpose": "Behind-the-scenes story of The Iron Snail's failed attempt to make a wool sweater in the US. It explains why domestic clothing manufacturing is hard for a small brand and lays out the company's new strategy.",
    "video_type": "explainer",
    "thesis": "Making clothing in the US is extremely risky for a small brand. Without government support or running fabric stock, the brand funds and carries the risk of every step of the supply chain. After a yarn dispute and cascading delays nearly bankrupted The Iron Snail, Michael created 'Operation Gumby' to restructure how the company manufactures.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "1,000 lb of raw wool sent to US spinners in May 2024 came back as yarn that torqued and snapped, and both knitters rejected it.",
      "The spinner ('Mr. Buck Wild') angrily refused a refund. A third-party lab later confirmed that two standard yarn treatments (anti-friction and stabilising) were never done, though the yarn itself was good.",
      "Countries that dominate clothing production support their manufacturers with subsidies, research labs, marketing and worker training. The US mainly offers the Berry Amendment for military contracts.",
      "When US trade protections dropped, the US lost the low-price end to low-cost countries and the luxury end to Japan and Italy. Niche heritage makers like Russell Moccasin and Horween survived.",
      "Japanese mills keep running fabrics ready to ship. Many US suppliers make everything to order, so the brand pays deposits up front and absorbs delays across sequential steps.",
      "Paying deposits for Billy Goat sweaters, denim and Mammoth jackets at the same time locked up all the company's money when every supply chain was delayed.",
      "Operation Gumby has four parts: keep good partners and go international to fill gaps; cut domestic orders by 75% but place them quarterly; send garments and yarns to NCSU's textile college to develop 'University Denim'; and eventually a 'hub' running fabrics for other manufacturers.",
      "Japanese spinners re-treated the yarn, the Billy Goat sweater project is back on, and a Buckwild beanie was introduced."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "The supply chain conflict",
        "summary": "The timeline of the wool yarn for the sweater, the knitters rejecting it, and the spinner's furious reply to a refund request. Michael proposes an independent lab test.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 4
      },
      {
        "title": "Sponsor: Unbound Merino",
        "summary": "Sponsor read for Unbound Merino wool t-shirts, sweaters, turtlenecks and base layers.",
        "first_segment": 4,
        "last_segment": 7
      },
      {
        "title": "The global textile disadvantage",
        "summary": "How other countries back their clothing manufacturers and how the end of US trade protection squeezed US makers on both price and luxury. Covers which heritage sectors survived and the role of the Berry Amendment.",
        "first_segment": 7,
        "last_segment": 12
      },
      {
        "title": "The recipe for disaster",
        "summary": "Michael's plan to fund Billy Goats, denim and Mammoth in sequence with simultaneous deposits. He contrasts running fabrics in Japan with make-to-order US supply chains, and shows how delays locked up funds and create boom-and-bust ordering.",
        "first_segment": 12,
        "last_segment": 19
      },
      {
        "title": "Lessons in leadership",
        "summary": "Three realisations: no one else will fix it, output beats hard work, and his parents' example of pushing through.",
        "first_segment": 20,
        "last_segment": 23
      },
      {
        "title": "Operation Gumby",
        "summary": "Courtney joins to reorganise operations, and Operation Firestorm focuses on denim. The four-part Operation Gumby plan is laid out.",
        "first_segment": 23,
        "last_segment": 28
      },
      {
        "title": "Resolution and next steps",
        "summary": "Lab results prove two yarn treatments were skipped, no refund ever came, and Japanese spinners re-treat the yarn. Billy Goats and a new Buckwild beanie are announced, ending with launch reactions.",
        "first_segment": 28,
        "last_segment": 32
      }
    ],
